[dmake]
<i>"I'd rather be a civilian and watch than a priest risking life and limb.<br>
--Nimbian ex-priest</i>

<p>Demos can add that extra spice into your campain...or you can just make one for fun.
<p>A demo is simpler than a full level.  Why?  Because it only needs the ~l[header]~w part of the english file ~i(the ai stuff)~w and the ~l[END]~w, plus one more thing....
<p>Under the header section add ~ldemo = 1~w and ~ldemoRestartTimer = time limit in seconds~w.  You also have to make the ~lai1~w information, but include the player info too.  The ~lai1~w tells how YOUR player will work.
<p>To replace the ~l[A.]~w heading you use ~l[Demo]~w.  As with the [A.] you enter the theme of the level and a button to start it or a timeout trigger.
<p>That's it!  It's done!


[switch]
<i>"It's as simple as a light switch....whatever that is."<br>
--Wardjan Franer, Inventor</i>
<p>Hello!  
<p>You have choosen something very intresting to study my friend.  Things can be enabled or disabled!  
<p>It's all done with this line:<br>
~mgts~l$Config,variable=0/1~mlts~w 
<p>and these two:<br>
~mgts~i?~l{variable}~mlts~l and whatever here like text and stuff ~mgts~l/?~mlts~w<br>
~mgts~i?!~l{variable}~mlts~l and whatever here like text and stuff ~mgts~l/?~mlts~w
<p>The difference is that the ~i?~w is run when the switch is ~l1~w<br>
While the one with the ~i!~w (~i?!~w) is run when the switch is ~l0~w
<p>Confused?  Don't worry it's quite simple.  The ~iconfig~w is basicly an ~lon/off~w switch or flag.  Where 0 is ~roff~w and 1 is ~lon~w.  It turns off or on the variable you give the switch, which can be a ~l.fort file~w ~i(toggles access to the level using donelevelname)~w.  It can also be a name that you wish ~i(perhaps like "hidden" to represent something revealed when something special is done.)~w
<p>Now the relevence of the other two lines is that's it's what you see if the switch is ~lon~w or ~roff~w  It's also kinda like a heading, but it's contained by ~l?'s~w  It begins with a ~mgts~l?~l{variable}~mlts~w.  Then it can be followed by text or other things that will be "~lhidden~w" untill the switch is ~lon~w.  To end the hidden heading use a ~mgts~l/?~mlts~w (Sorry about the ~mgts~l and the ~mlts~l, it's the only way I can do this so it will appear properly..for now) 
<p>~wOh yeah you need a ~l[init]~w to initialize variables and the ViewSpot.  If you want to use variables place them under the ~l[init]~w heading.  You only need to initialize them ~lonce!~w  The value is carried over to other levels, good for keeping track of what tech or allies you have.  ~i(thanx swelb)~w  


$Button=Back,Tell,event

[other]
<i>"Timing is everything...well it's important to say the least."<br>
--Grendor Clockworth, Nimbian cordinator</i>

<p>Here is some more intresting stuff:

<p>~l[@number in seconds]~w

<p>This basicly is the same as the timeout trigger, except for one detail...It works during game play.  Another thing about it that it is also a header, meaning that you can place other events under it.

<p>~mgts~l$ViewSpot,(~mxaxis~lx~myaxis~l)~mlts~w

<p>This centers the screen on the x, y cordinates you give it (works like an event)

<p>~lmyProd="......."~w

<p>Sets the production of your factories before the game starts. It uses the same Tech syntax as myTech="......"

<p>~mgts~l$MoveDialog,location~mlts~w

<p>This moves the message box to a certain part of the screen.  Where location is 2 characters a combination of 

<p>t = top<br>
m = middle<br>
b = bottom<br>
l = left<br>
c = center<br>
r = right

<p>So, if you wanted the Dialog box in the top right corner, you'd use:<br>

~mgts~l$MoveDialog,tr~mlts

$Button=Back,Tell,event

[impor]
<i>"Nooo!!! not AGAIN!!!"<br>
--Rahn, When I discover things wrong with campains.</i>

<p>
This is more of a convenece matter, but deserves attention anyway...
<p>There are quite a few campains out there you know.  My ~ld~w directory gets quite cluttered as well as my ~lcampain~w menu if I have too many campains and levels in there.  So I like to move campains in and out of the ~ld~w directory to keeps things under control.  Do you know how inconvent it is to move a campain with level names that are compleatly different out?  For our sake keep your campain names ~B~E~lCONSISTANT!~w~N~.  For example use names like campain1, campain2, campain3 and on.  If you do insist on making your level names different, give them a common first letter or number.  ~mThanx~w
<p>Another thing is ~lTEST~w your levels and correct any mistakes you find.  I people tend to hate mistakes in the level or campain.  Some don't know anything about making or fixing levels so that thems them in a bind.  The people who do know how to fix the mistakes don't tend to enjoy fixing the errors.  So please test your levels before you release them to the unsuspecting population.

$Button=Back,Tell,Menu

[text]
<i>"Ahh the rainbow after the storm..."<br>
--Unknown</i>  
<p>
Text formating is quite simple.  You just need to use a ~l~~~w before a certain letter that changes text.  You can also make text ~BBOLD~., ~Iitalic~. or ~Eembrossed~..  They can be ~B~I~ECOMBINED ~. with ~B~I~E~rC~oO~yL~gO~bR~mS  ~.So take your pick, just make sure you have the ~l~~~w after each letter.

<p>
~E~rr          ~ww<br>
~oo          ~tt<br>
~yy          ~jj<br>
~gg          ~qq<br>
~bb          ~xx<br>
~mm         ~kk<br>
            ~ii<br>
            ~ll<br>
~..  (normal color)
<p>
~NB = ~BBOLD<br>
~NI = ~Iitalic<br>
~NE = ~Eembrossed<br>
~NN = Normal<br>

$Button=Back,Tell,Menu



[exp]
<i>"One action is sure to be followed by another."<br>
--Book of Nimbus</i>  
<p>Ok you want to know about events?  To start off I'll use a simple example:
<p>[ai2PriestDead]<br>
<h2>The Priest has been sacraficed!</h2>
<p>
You watch as the priest goes up in flames, all the while screaming in agony...
<p>$ Button=Ok,DoNothing,0<br>
(no space between the $ and the Button)
<p>
Ok the important parts of this is the PriestDead and the button.
<p>
The ~lPriestDead~w part is the heading of the event.  Basicly it's an identifier. <br>
The ~lButton part~w is an example of an action.
<p>
To elaborate on the actions I have made 2 sections for it...but first more about the example shown.
<p>
~l$ Button=Ok~w (the trigger or start button)<br>
~l DoNothing,0~w (what the action acually does)

$Button=More about Triggers,Tell,star
$Button=More about Actions,Tell,act
$Button=Menu,Tell,event

